# What a set of documents does not answer, broken down by why. One row per (question, reason),
# with how many times that pairing occurs across whichever documents the caller chose.
#
# The scope is a slot
#   That is the point of the file. "Some relation, over some set of documents" is the shape of
#   every finding here, and choosing the document set is the single decision most easily
#   forgotten: forget it and a report about the world quietly counts the stipulations written to
#   exercise it. A slot cannot be forgotten, because a caller that supplies no `@scope` does not
#   compose at all. The choice moves from a habit into the build.
#
# Two callers, two questions
#   Over the corpus, this reads as what nobody has measured yet: the honest map of the evidence's
#   edges, and where the next instrument is worth building. Over the fixtures, it reads as what
#   this repository had to invent: every absence a stipulation had to assert because no real
#   filing produced it, which is the list of states the model can handle and has never actually
#   met.
-- epistemics/absences.sqlc, restricted by the caller's @scope.
SELECT a.question, a.reason, count(*) AS times
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/absences.sqlc)
) a
JOIN (
    :compose(@scope)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC, 1, 2
